= Sociology of knowledge — Simple =

The '''sociology of knowledge''' studies how society affects what people believe, accept, produce, and share as knowledge.

People do not learn and think in isolation. They participate in families, schools, professions, religions, scientific communities, governments, media systems, and other institutions. These environments influence which questions seem important, which experts are trusted, and which categories people use.

== Main idea ==

Suppose two communities study the same problem.

They may have different:

* instruments;
* vocabulary;
* training;
* institutions;
* historical experience;
* incentives.

A sociologist of knowledge can study how these differences affect the claims the communities produce.

This does **not** automatically tell us which claim is true. Explaining why people believe something is different from testing whether the belief is well supported.

== Historical development ==

Émile Durkheim and Marcel Mauss studied relationships between social organization and systems of classification.

Max Scheler and Karl Mannheim helped establish the modern sociology of knowledge in the early twentieth century. Mannheim became especially influential through ''Ideology and Utopia'', which examined relationships between social position, ideology, and styles of thought.<ref name="mannheim">[https://www.routledge.com/Ideology-and-Utopia-An-Introduction-to-the-Sociology-of-Knowledge/Mannheim/p/book/9780415060547 Mannheim, K., ''Ideology and Utopia''.]</ref>

Peter Berger and Thomas Luckmann later widened the field to everyday life in ''The Social Construction of Reality''.<ref name="berger">[https://books.google.com/books/about/The_Social_Construction_of_Reality.html?id=rGg9mT_JNIEC Berger, P. L. and Luckmann, T. (1966), ''The Social Construction of Reality''.]</ref>

== Social construction ==

Some parts of social reality exist because people repeatedly create and maintain them.

Money, professional credentials, legal offices, institutional roles, and many classifications depend on shared practices and rules.

Calling something socially constructed does not mean it is imaginary.

A legal institution can be socially constructed and still have very real effects. A medical category can have a social history while also referring to biological patterns.

The important question is: '''what exactly is being constructed, and through which social processes?'''

== Everyday knowledge ==

Most social knowledge is not formal theory.

People learn:

* how institutions work;
* what behavior is expected;
* whom to trust;
* how language classifies situations;
* what counts as normal.

Berger and Luckmann emphasized this taken-for-granted knowledge of everyday life.

== Science as a social institution ==

Science also has social organization:

* laboratories;
* journals;
* peer review;
* universities;
* funding;
* professional societies;
* research communities.

The sociology of scientific knowledge studies how these institutions influence scientific work.

That does not mean scientific evidence is only a social opinion. Researchers can study both the social process and the quality of the evidence.

== Experts and authority ==

Modern societies depend on experts.

Sociologists can ask:

* How does someone become recognized as an expert?
* Which institutions grant credentials?
* How are disagreements handled?
* Why are some experts trusted more than others?

Expert authority can be useful and justified, but experts remain fallible.

== Knowledge and power ==

Institutions can influence which knowledge becomes visible.

A government may create official categories. A university may decide which research receives funding. A platform may rank some information above other information.

These choices can shape public knowledge without determining whether every resulting claim is true.

== Sociology versus epistemology ==

[[Epistemology]] asks questions such as: What is knowledge? What makes a belief justified?

The sociology of knowledge asks: How do social conditions influence beliefs, claims, and institutions?

Social epistemology connects these questions by studying how social practices affect justified belief.<ref name="sep">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology-social/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Social Epistemology.”]</ref>

